      'Raag Shivranjani is traditionally performed during the late evening and midnight hours (second and third Prahars of the night, roughly 9:00 PM to 1:00 AM). Its dominant aesthetic mood is Karuna Rasa (poignant pathos, tender grief, and deep human empathy) intertwined with Vipralambha Shringar (the ache of separation) and devotional surrender (Bhakti).',
      'Why does the five-note sequence `S R g P D` touch the human heart so instantaneously, even for listeners with zero formal training in classical music? Acoustically, the lower three notes (`S – R – g`) form a compact minor trichord ending in a half-step (`R–g`), while the upper notes (`P – D – S\'`) form a bright, open major sixth (`D`) and tonic (`S\'`). Whenever the flutist ascends `S R g P D S\'`, the bright Shuddha Dhaivat (`D`) offers a moment of luminous hope; then, as the melody descends from Pancham across the wide major-third interval down to half-holed Komal Gandhar (`D P ~> g R S`), that hope melts back into wistful acceptance.',
